;;; A rule's `:assert (absent (paid ?g))` stores a fact of `absent` —
;;; M1f **W80**, register row `W80/absent-concluded`, filed by S1f.7.7 (T1f.7.7.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, of whom Ann has paid, and a rule that concludes `(absent (paid ?g))`
;;; of every guest. No candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No conclusion is headed `absent`: refused at the parse, on every command. A rule
;;; that concludes a guest has not paid writes `(not (paid ?g))`, and this one would
;;; then kill root, since Ann has paid.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `52e05fc1`, release: the program loads and the rule stores `(absent (paid Ann))` and
;;; `(absent (paid Bob))` beside `(paid Ann)`; root is alive and the goal answers
;;; `?g = Ann`, exit 0 on every command.
;;; Refused at load since S1f.7.7 (T1f.7.7.3).

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(paid Ann)
(rule unpaid () :match (is-a ?g Guest) :assert (absent (paid ?g)) :why "{?g} has not paid")
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
(query :goal (paid ?g))
